          "text": "$(-\\infty, 7)$"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "$[1, 7]$"
        },
        {
          "label": "E",
          "text": "$(1, 7)$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "D",
      "lessonIds": [
        "L29"
      ],
      "sourcePage": 11,
      "solution": "**Note on this question:** As printed, the sum starts at $n=0$, where the denominator $n^5 3^n$ is $0$, so its first term is undefined. The intended reading starts at $n=1$, which gives the keyed interval $[1,7]$: the Ratio Test gives radius $3$ about $x=4$, and both endpoints converge absolutely, since there $\\left|\\frac{(x-4)^n}{n^5 3^n}\\right|=\\frac{1}{n^5}$.",
      "status": "reviewed"
    },
